Below is a **self‑contained console application** that shows two common ways you can get parallelism in C#:

1. **I/O‑bound work** – several HTTP downloads are started at the same time using `Task`s and `async/await`.  
2. **CPU‑bound work** – a large array is processed with a `Parallel.For` loop (you could equally use PLINQ).

The code is fully commented so you can see what each part is doing and you can copy‑paste it into a .NET 6+ project (or any newer .NET Core/Framework that supports top‑level statements).

```csharp
// Program.cs
//  .NET 6+ – top‑level statements are used for brevity
//  Compile with: dotnet new console -n TaskParallelDemo
//  Copy this file into Program.cs, then run: dotnet run

using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using System.Diagnostics;
using System.Linq;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  I/O‑bound parallelism – multiple downloads in parallel
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

/// <summary>
/// Download a string from a given URL asynchronously.
/// The method returns a Task<string> that completes when the download finishes.
/// </summary>
static async Task<string> DownloadStringAsync(string url, CancellationToken ct = default)
{
    using var http = new HttpClient();
    // If the token is canceled the request will throw a TaskCanceledException
    return await http.GetStringAsync(url, ct);
}

static async Task DownloadManyAsync()
{
    // Example URLs – replace with any URLs you like
    var urls = new[]
    {
        "https://www.example.com",
        "https://www.microsoft.com",
        "https://www.github.com",
        "https://www.stackoverflow.com"
    };

    var cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
    // In a real app you could cancel after some timeout or user input:
    // cts.CancelAfter(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10));

    var stopwatch = Stopwatch.StartNew();

    try
    {
        // Start one Task for each URL – all run concurrently
        var downloadTasks = urls.Select(u => DownloadStringAsync(u, cts.Token)).ToArray();

        // Wait until ALL downloads complete (or any throws/cancel)
        var results = await Task.WhenAll(downloadTasks);

        stopwatch.Stop();

        Console.WriteLine($"Downloaded {urls.Length} pages in {stopwatch.ElapsedMilliseconds} ms");
        foreach (var (url, content) in urls.Zip(results, (u, c) => (u, c)))
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"--- {url} (len={content.Length}) ---");
        }
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Download operation was canceled.");
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Something went wrong: {ex.Message}");
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  CPU‑bound parallelism – processing a large array in parallel
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

static void ProcessLargeArray()
{
    const int arraySize = 10_000_000;
    int[] numbers = Enumerable.Range(1, arraySize).ToArray();

    // We want to compute the sum of all numbers – this is CPU bound.
    // Parallel.For lets the runtime split the work across all logical cores.
    long sum = 0;                         // use a long to avoid overflow
    var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew();

    // Parallel loop – each iteration adds a number to the shared sum.
    // Interlocked is used to make the addition thread‑safe.
    Parallel.For(0, numbers.Length, i =>
    {
        Interlocked.Add(ref sum, numbers[i]);
    });

    sw.Stop();
    Console.WriteLine($"Sum of 1..{arraySize} = {sum} computed in {sw.ElapsedMilliseconds} ms (parallel)");
    Console.WriteLine($"(Serial would be {arraySize * (arraySize + 1) / 2})");
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Main entry point
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

Console.WriteLine("=== Task (async I/O) Parallelism Demo ===");
await DownloadManyAsync();

Console.WriteLine("\n=== Parallel.For (CPU) Demo ===");
ProcessLargeArray();

Console.WriteLine("\nPress any key to exit...");
Console.ReadKey();
```

### What this program shows

| Feature | How it works | When to use it |
|---------|--------------|----------------|
| **`Task.WhenAll`** | Launches several *asynchronous* operations (e.g., HTTP requests) and awaits all of them together. | I/O‑bound work where the threads can be free while the I/O completes. |
| **`async/await`** | Makes asynchronous code look like synchronous code; the compiler turns the method into a state machine that returns a `Task`. | Any operation that already provides `Task`‑based APIs (`HttpClient.GetStringAsync`, `File.ReadAllTextAsync`, etc.). |
| **`Parallel.For`** | Executes a `for` loop concurrently across all available processor cores. | CPU‑bound calculations where you can split the work into independent chunks. |
| **`Interlocked`** | Provides atomic operations on shared variables, ensuring thread safety when many tasks update the same value. | Shared state in parallel loops. |
| **`CancellationTokenSource`** | Lets you cancel running tasks. | Long‑running or user‑interruptible operations. |

Feel free to modify the URLs, array size, or add more complex processing logic to see how parallelism scales with your workload. Happy coding!
